From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:57:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 32/45] hjviewer: same-account login: the controller logs in
 with its account key

A controller logged in to the account proves itself to the account's PCs with
its account key instead of asking for the PC's password (design v3 3-1, 3-4,
3-9, 1-6). Four hooks in client.rs, the rest in HJViewer's files:

- Client::start notes the PC key its channel was checked against, for a
  secured channel, under the session's ID. The connections of a session share
  that ID and can run at once (on desktop the file transfer, camera, terminal
  and port forwarding opened from a session's toolbar), so a session keeps
  every such key of the last 24 hours, longer than any channel waits for its
  login, and a login carries a proof only when all of them are the key the
  account bound to the PC.
- handle_hash asks hjviewer_account::key_proof, after the terminal admin
  branch. With a proof the login goes out as create_login_msg makes it, with
  the remembered password if there is one and the proof added, and no
  password box comes first. When the PC's key is not the one the account's
  device list (hjv-my-devices) has for it, an 'error' box warns and the
  connection ends: no proof, no password. Otherwise handle_hash goes on as
  before.
- handle_login_error: "Empty Password" after a proof means the PC refused it;
  the 're-input-password' box says so, and its [Retry] opens the password box.

The proof goes only to a PC in the device list that is not needs-relogin, on a
secured channel, for the remote screen, file transfer, camera or the terminal
as far as the list's kinds allow, never over an id@server connection, never
for port forwarding or RDP, and only with the PC's 32-byte nonce, so a PC
without the feature never gets a lone proof to wait on. The key continuity
check runs before the nonce and the kinds, and for port forwarding and RDP
too: a PC that leaves its nonce out, or a port forwarding to it, does not get
the remembered password around it. The device list, the key serial and the
login are read through api.rs, which keeps them, so the controller reads the
list in the format the account requests write it.

The boxes' texts are in Korean and English, picked by the app's language
(mod.rs korean(), shared with the PC's connection manager name): its own
setting, else the system's. Phones get sys-locale for the latter
(Cargo.toml); it was a desktop-only dependency, and ctl.rs builds everywhere.

A build without HJV_SERVER, HJV_LOGIN_SEAL_PUBKEY or HJV_TRUST_PUBKEYS keeps
1.5.1's path: the hooks return at once.

Tests: the conditions as a table, the proof against the shared vectors (and
accepted by the PC's check), no proof without a nonce, the key warning for the
account's PCs only, for any channel of the session and for port forwarding,
and connections through the client's own secured handshake to a PC on
loopback that checks proofs as design 3-5 has it: the account's PC opens
without a password box and gets the login create_login_msg makes plus the
proof; a refused proof gets the refusal box, or goes in by the remembered
password; a PC without the nonce, another account's PC, port forwarding to the
PC under its bound key and a needs-relogin PC under another key get 1.5.1's
login; a changed key warns and sends nothing, for port forwarding too; without
the feature the hooks do nothing.

Known limits: the terminal's administrator login asks for the Windows
credentials before handle_hash reaches the hook, and a password typed into a
port forwarding window's prompt answers its other mappings' Hash directly
(login_from_ui), so, as in 1.5.1, neither gets the key check. A channel whose
login comes more than 24 hours after its handshake has no key noted and goes
the password way without the check, and so does, as in 1.5.1, a channel that
failed the key handshake and that the user lets go on unencrypted. A session
that noted the PC's earlier key within the last 24 hours (a window that
connects again after the PC was installed anew and bound again) shows the key
warning and closes; a new connection goes through.

(Design v3, work package C3: patch 0032.)

